  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap integral C\u2085 for a step function h under specific constraints. The challenge lies in efficiently navigating the non-convex optimization landscape to find a near-optimal solution within the given time and resource constraints. The key insights guiding this approach are:\n\n- The problem can be reformulated to minimize the maximum cross-correlation between h and (1 - h) across all shifts.\n- Exact gradient computation is more efficient and accurate than finite differences.\n- Utilizing the Adam optimizer leverages adaptive learning rates for faster convergence.\n- Maintaining feasibility through careful clipping and scaling ensures constraints are met.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe aim to minimize:\n$$ C_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] (1 - h[(i + k) \\% n]) \\right) $$\nsubject to:\n$$ 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\quad \\forall i $$\n$$ \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] = \\frac{n}{2} $$\n\nWhere n is the number of points (n_points=100). The exact gradient for each h[i] is derived based on the current maximum cross-correlation shift k.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n   i. Seed the random number generator for reproducibility.\n   ii. If initial_h_values exist, interpolate to n_points and normalize.\n   iii. Otherwise, initialize h to a uniform distribution scaled to sum(n/2).\n\nb. Optimization loop:\n   i. For each iteration, compute the exact gradient by identifying the shift k with the maximum cross-correlation.\n   ii. Update h using the Adam optimizer, adjusting learning rate based on convergence rate.\n   iii. Clip h to [0, 1] and rescale it to maintain the sum constraint.\n   iv. Track the best solution found.\n\nc. Termination:\n   i. Stop when the time budget is exhausted or no improvement is detected.\n   ii. Return the best feasible solution found.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- n_points=100 balances accuracy and computational efficiency.\n- Adam optimizer with adaptive learning rates accelerates convergence.\n- Exact gradient computation ensures efficient updates.\n- Clipping and rescaling maintain feasibility.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach iteration processes exact gradients in O(n\u00b2), with 1000 iterations expected within 600s. The algorithm includes safeguards to handle numerical instability and ensures feasible solutions at each step.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks verify h values and sum constraints. The best solution is validated against the evaluator to ensure correctness. The program returns the best h, its C\u2085, and n_points.",
